[W-ARROW-FORALL] Warning: this statement is written as a function type: `∀` is for a statement
    ╭─[ reject/match-scrutinee-from-lemma-under-lambda.sw:20:40 ]
    │
 20 │ theorem gIs (ok: Nat → Bool) (x: Nat): (rest: List Nat) → g ok x rest = anyR (ys => all2 ok (Cons x ys)) rest
    │                                        ────────┬───────  
    │                                                ╰───────── write `∀ (…) =>` instead of `(…) ->`
    │ 
    │ Note: a statement quantified over a variable is `∀ (x: T) => P`; `(x: T) -> V` is for a function type whose result is a type, and `A -> B` for an implication whose hypothesis is unnamed. The quick fix "Write ∀ instead of ->" does it
────╯
[E-REFL] Error: `rfl` can't prove this
    ╭─[ reject/match-scrutinee-from-lemma-under-lambda.sw:26:40 ]
    │
 26 │     | True => rewrite gIs ok x rest in rfl
    │                                        ─┬─  
    │                                         ╰─── `anyR (ys => ok x && all2 ok ys) rest` and `anyR (ys => all2 ok ys) rest` aren't equal
    │ 
    │ Note 1: `rfl` proves `a = b` only when both sides compute to the same value
    │ 
    │ Note 2: `ok x` is inside a function here, where `step: ok x` isn't used: `rfl` and `cases` compute, but don't rewrite with a hypothesis. This `ok x` came in after the `match` on it (from a lemma's statement or a term written in the branch), so the `match` didn't replace it. `rewrite step in …` does, as `ok x` doesn't mention the function's parameter
